                <pb facs="0731" n="661" sameAs="frus:frus1867p2/pg661"/>

                <head>[Untitled]</head>
                <p>The Phare de la Loire publishes the following letter, which is circulating in
                    Paris :</p>
                <p rendition="#center">TO THE DEMOCRATS.</p>
                <p>The Mexicans have tried and executed Maximilian. French democrats have already
                    expressed the wish that Juarez might pardon his prisoner. We will wait, however,
                    for monarchists, old and new, to accuse us of urging the act, according to our
                    bloody customs.</p>
                <pb facs="0732" n="662" xml:id="pg662"/>
                <p>We will put these two questions to the opponents of democracy:</p>
                <p>1st. When the police, in contempt of the law of nations, caught the Duke of
                    Enghien abroad and brought him to France, did Bonaparte the first consul spare
                    his life?</p>
                <p>The ditches of Vincennes have the answer.</p>
                <p>2d. When the King Murat reclaimed his throne in Naples, did his <hi
                        rendition="#italic">lucky rival</hi> the Bourbon pardon him ? The
                    Neapolitans yet remember the bloody day of the 13th October, 1815.</p>
                <p>And yet Enghein thought he had the hereditary right of his ancestors on his side,
                    and Murat could allege a recent possession of the crown.</p>
                <p>Accept the assurance, &amp;c.,</p>
                <p rendition="#right">* * *</p>
            </div>
